{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים נַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם וגו׳</b>. The intention in this is that HaShem did not create man like the creation of the swarming creatures of the waters and the swarming creatures of the earth, concerning which He said to the waters וְהִשְׁרִיצוּ and He said to the earth וְהוֹצִיאָה; rather, He — the Creator — said that He Himself would make man." "And He said <b>נַעֲשֶׂה</b> because He is the source of humility; therefore He spoke in plural language, for thus would a humble individual speak, and He did not concern Himself with giving room for error, because He prefaced it by saying ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים״ — a singular Name. HaShem — He is the Elohim — He in His glory and in His essence, as it were, gathered up his dust and breathed into him a living spirit. It further intends, in His saying <b>נַעֲשֶׂה</b> in plural language, that the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He, are many: the thirteen attributes of mercy and the Name Elohim, which is the attribute of judgment, agreed together to create man." "And His saying <b>בְּצַלְמֵנוּ כִּדְמוּתֵנוּ</b> — it is possible that He intends to say that there should be in him both the aspect of mercy and the aspect of judgment, to activate through them the ways of judgment and the ways of mercy as appropriate — and understand this. And it is the sod of His saying (Genesis 2:7) ״וַיִּיצֶר ה׳ אֱלֹהִים״ וגו׳. And His saying <b>וְיִרְדּוּ</b> agrees with our interpretation, in that the creation is in likeness to the Creator in the aspects of mercy and judgment: through judgment it is that he shall rule over the creatures, for he possesses the aspect of mercy toward the worthy and those in need of it, and to put to death one liable to judgment. And He granted him dominion over all; and although He did not mention dominion over the waters, it is alluded to in His saying ״בִּדְגַת הַיָּם״ — behold, He mentioned the sea among the things to be ruled." "And by way of remez, He hinted in His using the language of descent, along the lines of what our masters of blessed memory informed us (Sefer HaGilgulim): through the midst of a person's deeds he descends in his level from the aspect of man to the aspect of fish and to the aspect of birds and to the aspect of beast and to the aspect of creeping things — according to the measure of the sin, a person is lowered from his level of excellence until he descends lower and lower, may the Merciful One save us. And this is what He hinted here at the time of creation: the gradations of descent through which he will receive his punishment, and also that through their midst he will return to his root. And He prefaced by speaking of the descent of a human soul to the level of fish, for the descent designated for exalted souls when they sin is that their soul descends to be reincarnated in fish, and therefore Scripture assigned to them in their place ״אֲסִיפָה״ (gathering), for they have no suffering through the distress of slaughter by sword, but their gathering purges them (Ḥulin 27a). And afterward He spoke of the descent lesser than that, which is the reincarnation of the soul in birds, and this requires more distress than fish, and HaShem blessed be He commanded for it half-slaughter, as that which we learned: רוֹב אֶחָד בָּעוֹף — one majority in a bird. And afterward He spoke of descent in beasts, which is a lesser aspect still and whose distress is greater, as that which we learned (ibid.): רוֹב שְׁנַיִם בַּבְּהֵמָה — two majorities in a beast. And afterward He spoke of a descent that is in the silent (inanimate) and the growing (vegetative), and that is His saying <b>וּבְכָל הָאָרֶץ</b>: the word ״הָאָרֶץ״ tells of the silent, and the word ״בְּכָל״ alludes within it to the growing. And all these are descents of souls in which there exists the hope of repair to return to their former state, for from the silent he will ascend to the growing, and from the growing he will enter a living creature that does not speak, and from a living creature that does not speak he will ascend to a living creature that speaks. And there is yet a still lesser descent, which is annihilation after which there is no hope, and that is descent to the aspect of שְׁקָצִים וּרְמָשִׂים — detestable and creeping things. This one — his hope is lost, and they are the aspects of the wicked who exchange their faith, the transgressors of Israel whose deeds have surpassed all bounds."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:57:16.967219063Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}